        heading: "Ramp Slope, Alley Geometry, and Trailer Fit",
        body: "The engineer sets the ramp slope from animal safety: gentle enough that cattle walk up without rushing or slipping, typically well under 20 degrees, with the exact number matched to the rise the site requires. Cleats, grooves, or rubber surfacing give footing in all weather, and solid sides keep cattle focused forward. The approach alley's curve is drawn with a radius cattle accept — not a hairpin — and the engineer keeps the width consistent from staging pen to ramp so there is no pinch point where the flow stops.\n\nTrailer fit is confirmed with the haulers, not assumed. The engineer checks deck heights for the pot-belly and straight-deck trailers the yard ships on, designs the dock or adjustable ramp to meet them with minimal step, and confirms the dock width and gate arrangement against the trailer's compartments. Overhead clearance, dock bumpers, and the gate the crew works from the catwalk are all detailed on the plans, because the loadout gets used in the dark, in winter, and in a hurry.",
