    title: "How Is Feedlot Drainage Designed for Stormwater Control?",
    description: "Drainage engineering separates clean upslope water from pen runoff with diversions, graded surfaces, and culverts sized for design storms, meeting county rules.",
    h1: "How Is Feedlot Drainage Designed for Stormwater Control?",
    answer: "The engineering answer is that feedlot drainage is two systems in one: the clean-water system that keeps upslope runoff from ever touching the lots, and the contaminated-water system that collects what falls on the production area and delivers it to the runoff containment. Mixing them wastes pond capacity; separating them is the whole design. Direct answer: the engineer diverts clean water around the site with ditches and berms, grades pens and alleys at two to four percent toward collection, sizes channels and culverts for the design storm, crowns roads to shed water, and protects every outlet against erosion — with county floodplain and grading permits reconciled.\n\nClean-water diversion is designed first. The engineer maps the contributing watershed above the feedlot and cuts it off with diversion ditches, berms, or terraces that carry upslope water around pens, alleys, stockpiles, and buildings to a natural outlet. The diversions are sized for the design storm — commonly the 25-year event — because a diversion that overtops sends clean water into the contaminated system and blows the pond sizing. Outlets are armored where concentrated flow meets erodible soil.\n\nThe production-area collection is graded as one hydraulic network. Pen outlets, alley drains, and the areas around bunks, waterers, and the loadout all drain toward the settling basin and retention pond, and the engineer checks the grading plan for the low spots and backfalls that create permanent mud. Channels are sized for the design storm with freeboard; culverts under roads and alleys are sized so the road is not the dam — an undersized culvert floods the alley it was meant to drain. The engineer details erosion protection at every concentrated outlet: riprap, concrete flumes, or turf reinforcement where flow meets bare ground.\n\nRoads and structures get their own drainage detailing. The engineer crowns feedlot roads so water sheets off both sides into ditches, sizes the roadside ditches for the design storm, and provides culverts at every crossing. Building drip lines and roof runoff are collected and routed — clean roof water can go to the clean system, but only if it is truly kept separate. Where the site sits in or near a mapped floodplain, the engineer reconciles the design with the county floodplain development permit: no net fill in the floodway, structures elevated or protected per the ordinance, and the permit in hand before grading starts.",
